cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) models economic and organizational activity through
  a physical or real-resource state K, actor-specific exchange-value and capital
  representations K_i, structured subjective states P_i, and actor-side action
  or process records A_i. The field is not an independent scalar value; it is a
  derived action-generating configuration of resources, subjective states,
  capital representations, and projection-specified relations and constraints.
  VFT separates feasible action sets from inducible or behaviorally available
  action sets, with the latter constrained to the former and defined ex ante by
  projection-specific decision structure. Multi-actor realized events are linked
  across actor-side records by shared event identity and participant relations,
  allowing shared event histories to be constructed without double counting.
  Physical endpoint change is represented through a projection-defined difference
  operator rather than universal subtraction. Decision-relevant forecasts are
  represented as forecasts of projection-selected outcome bundles that may include
  physical resource change, capital positions, realized-use outcomes, subjective
  states, and activity-continuity outcomes. VFT distinguishes physical capability,
  realized use experience, and exchange-value representation: VFT-specific
  use-value quantity is an interval-indexed realized subjective outcome, while
  exchange-value may describe both point-in-time positions and interval events.
  Formal accounting is treated as a projection rather than a universal causal
  layer. Exchange-value residuals may receive projection-specific interpretations
  such as surplus, profit, income, or valuation gain. VFT defines a business entity
  as a local action-generating field structure that continuously induces and
  reproduces activity, allowing firms, NPOs, public businesses, and state or
  municipal activities to be described without making profit maximization the
  universal definition of business. Entrepreneurship is represented as a type of
  business-oriented field formation. VFT retains resource-realization,
  activity-flow, and P-downside as constitutive rationality assumptions, with
  projection-specific admissibility, exclusion, or comparison rules required for
  their use in decision models. Marxian categories such as use-value, labor,
  exchange-value, surplus, and accumulation can be represented through
  projection-specific specializations.
